Which kinds of test-running stanza each dune file has: presence, not tallies. A tally here
moved whenever a test was added anywhere in the repository, so the numbers go to stderr
instead (gh-ocannl-665). What they guarded is guarded still: a line below changes when a
directory gains its first stanza of a kind or loses its last, and within a directory the
raw-text floor reported by the claim at the end holds the count up.

.claude/dune: nothing that runs a test executable
arrayjit/lib/dune: exempt rules (gen_builtins/generate.exe)
arrayjit/lib/gen_builtins/dune: nothing that runs a test executable
arrayjit/test/dune: tests, exe-running rules
benchmarks/dune: exempt rules (python3, handed %{dep:test_orchestrate.py})
benchmarks/runners/ocannl/dune: exempt rules (metal_queue_probe.exe)
bin/dune: exe-running rules
dune: nothing that runs a test executable
lib/dune: nothing that runs a test executable
tensor/dune: nothing that runs a test executable
test/config/dune: exe-running rules
test/einsum/dune: tests, inline-test libraries, exe-running rules
test/gpt2/dune: tests, exe-running rules
test/operations/atomic_file_rename_scan_fixture/dune: tests, exe-running rules
test/operations/dune: tests, inline-test libraries, exe-running rules
test/operations/profiles/dune: tests, exe-running rules
test/operations/startup_streams/dune: tests, exe-running rules
test/ppx/dune: tests, exe-running rules, exempt rules (pp.exe)
test/support/dune: nothing that runs a test executable
test/training/dune: tests, exe-running rules
tools/dune: nothing that runs a test executable

Stanza kinds that can run a test executable: rule, alias (plus `test`, `tests` and a `library`'s `inline_tests`, which dune runs itself).
Stanza kinds that cannot: executable, executables, copy_files, copy_files#, install, env, dirs, data_only_dirs, vendored_dirs, include_subdirs, documentation, ocamllex, ocamlyacc, menhir, toplevel, deprecated_library_name. Anything else fails above.
Actions that execute a program: run, dynamic-run, system, bash.
Actions that do not: progn, concurrent, chdir, setenv, with-stdout-to, with-stderr-to, with-outputs-to, with-stdin-from, with-accepted-exit-codes, ignore-stdout, ignore-stderr, ignore-outputs, no-infer, echo, write-file, cat, copy, copy#, copy-and-add-line-directive, diff, diff?, cmp, pipe-stdout, pipe-stderr, pipe-outputs, format-dune-file, or, and, not. Anything else fails above.

Exempt from the dependency, running an executable that reads no configuration:
  arrayjit/lib:gen_builtins/generate.exe -- the builtin header generator links only the OCaml stdlib and a pure C definition table; it reads no OCANNL configuration
  test/ppx:pp.exe -- the ppx driver, run to expand a source file and diff the expansion: `ppx_ocannl` links base, ppxlib, str and einsum_parser -- no configuration reader -- so no `ocannl_config` can reach its output
  benchmarks/runners/ocannl:metal_queue_probe.exe -- the standalone Metal queue probe, run on `@bin-smoke` as the bindings' runtime canary (gh-ocannl-905): it links `metal`, `ctypes` and `unix` and no arrayjit at all -- no configuration reader, so no `ocannl_config` can reach what it does
  benchmarks:python3, handed %{dep:test_orchestrate.py} -- the benchmark orchestrator's own unit tests, in Python: the interpreter runs a script that imports orchestrate.py and runners/bench_common.py and calls no OCANNL executable, so there is no configuration in reach -- and a Python process would not read one anyway

the scan places every test stanza, inline-test library and run executable the dune sources show: true
that floor applies to more than one dune file: true

OK: every test stanza, inline-test library and exe-running rule in these files declares ocannl_config, apart from the sites exempted above.

